GUI modernization, Phase 3 (frontend slice): real URL-based routing
(#/<page>?<InvestigationContext>), replacing App.tsx's local
useState<Page>. ADR 0001 originally chose "no router yet ... a dependency
should earn its place" for the five-page first slice; eight pages and a
real need for deep links/bookmarks/shareable investigation state later,
one has.

## 1. What changed

`desktop/src/routing/useRoute.ts`: a small hand-written hash router (no
dependency) - `parseRoute`/`buildHash` are pure functions,
`useRoute()` wraps them with `hashchange` listening and a `navigate`
callback. Defines `InvestigationContext` (`from, to, entity, severities,
families, query, sort, selection`) per the execution order's §4.2 - the
contract future page rebuilds (Phase 3's remaining work, Phase 5) write
against, not yet consumed by any page today (none of them read filter/time
-range state yet; that lands with each page's own rebuild).

App.tsx: `useState<Page>` → `useRoute()`; every `page === "..."` becomes
`route.page === "..."`; `Sidebar`'s `onNavigate` and the launch-options
effect's page-setting both call `navigate` instead of `setPage`.

## 2. Verified, not just typechecked

    $ npx tsc --noEmit                    clean
    $ npm test                            23/23 (15 existing + 8 new
                                           parseRoute/buildHash unit tests)
    $ npm run build                       95.06KB gzip JS (was 94.71KB -
                                           the router itself, ~0.3KB)
    $ npx playwright test --project=chromium --reporter=list
      32 passed (29 existing + 3 new routing tests)
    $ cargo test --lib ...                8/8 (untouched)

New Playwright tests (`tests/visual/routing.spec.ts`): clicking sidebar
items updates the URL hash; opening a URL with a hash already set
(`/#/rules`) lands directly on that page with the correct nav item marked
active - a real deep link, not the app ignoring the hash and always
opening Overview; browser back/forward actually moves between previously
visited pages.

Manually verified in a live browser session (not only Playwright):
clicking "Rules" produced `location.href ===
"http://localhost:1420/#/rules"`; navigating directly to
`http://localhost:1420/#/evidence` (simulating a pasted link or bookmark)
opened the Evidence bundles page directly, with it correctly marked active
in the sidebar - screenshot confirms both the correct page content and the
active nav state.

One test assumption corrected during verification, not a bug: the very
first load with an empty hash resolves to Overview internally
(`parseRoute("")`'s default) without ever calling `navigate()` to write
that default into the visible URL - by design, so opening the app does not
create a redundant history entry for a page nothing actually navigated to.
The hash only appears once real navigation happens.

## 3. Not yet done

No page reads `InvestigationContext` fields yet - time range, entity,
filters, sort, and selection are all defined on the type and carried
correctly through the URL round-trip, but nothing consumes them. That is
each page's own Phase 3/5 rebuild (Overview/Incidents/Timeline getting a
real time-range picker and filters; Investigation/Host reading `entity`;
incident chain links writing `selection` to deep-link into Timeline).
`--route` (the launch-option extension mentioned in the execution order)
is not implemented on the Rust side yet - `--page` continues to work as
it did.
